# Playwright Fixtures

Specs import `test` and `expect` from a project fixtures module, not from `@playwright/test`. Fixtures construct page objects; specs do not call `new SomePage(page)`.

## Layout

- Fixtures: `fixtures/index.ts` (or `fixtures/test.ts`)
- Page objects stay in `pages/**/*.ts`
- Auth state (when needed): a worker-scoped fixture writes to
  `<project.outputDir>/.auth/<parallelIndex>.json` and overrides the built-in
  `storageState` fixture — see the login-once-per-worker example below.

## Rules

- Extend the base test with typed fixtures that wrap page objects.
- Keep fixture setup/teardown next to the fixture; do not duplicate it in every spec.
- Prefer worker-scoped `storageState` for logged-in roles instead of UI login in each test.
- Implement "log in once" as a worker-scoped fixture inside the fixtures module
  (see example). Do **not** reach for a separate `*.setup.ts` file plus a
  dependent Playwright project for this — it adds config and an extra
  "test" that isn't actually a test, for something a fixture already handles.
- Each test still gets an isolated `page` / context; do not share `page` across tests.
- If tests under a logged-in role mutate shared state (cart, wishlist, profile,
  etc.), do not log every worker into the **same** account — parallel workers
  (and other people/CI runs hitting a shared demo site) will contend for that
  state and cause flaky failures. Seed a fresh, disposable account per worker
  instead (see the registration example below).

## Example: log in once per worker

```typescript
// fixtures/index.ts
import { existsSync, mkdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import { dirname, resolve } from 'node:path';
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoginPage } from '../pages/LoginPage';

type MyWorkerFixtures = {
  workerStorageStatePath: string;
};

export const test = base.extend<{}, MyWorkerFixtures>({
  storageState: async ({ workerStorageStatePath }, use) => {
    await use(workerStorageStatePath);
  },
  workerStorageStatePath: [
    async ({ browser }, use, workerInfo) => {
      const fileName = resolve(
        workerInfo.project.outputDir,
        `.auth/${workerInfo.parallelIndex}.json`,
      );

      if (!existsSync(fileName)) {
        const page = await browser.newPage({
          baseURL: workerInfo.project.use.baseURL,
          storageState: undefined,
        });
        const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);
        await loginPage.goto();
        await loginPage.login(process.env.TEST_USER_EMAIL!, process.env.TEST_USER_PASSWORD!);
        await expect(loginPage.headerLoginLink).toBeHidden();

        mkdirSync(dirname(fileName), { recursive: true });
        await page.context().storageState({ path: fileName });
        await page.close();
      }

      await use(fileName);
    },
    { scope: 'worker' },
  ],
});

export { expect };
```

Notes:

- `browser.newPage()` does not inherit the project's `use` options — pass
  `baseURL` explicitly so page objects can still `goto('/login')`.
- Caching on `existsSync(fileName)` means the login only runs the first time
  each worker needs it; later tests on that worker reuse the file.
- `<project.outputDir>` (e.g. `test-results/<project>/`) is already gitignored,
  so no extra `.gitignore`/eslint-ignore entries are needed for the auth file.

## Example: seed a fresh account per worker

Same worker-scoped `storageState` shape as above, but instead of logging into
one shared account, register a new disposable account per worker. This is the
right call whenever the logged-in flow under test mutates shared state (e.g. a
shopping cart) — each worker gets its own private account, so parallel runs
(and other traffic against a shared demo site) can never step on each other:

```typescript
// fixtures/index.ts
import { existsSync, mkdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import { dirname, resolve } from 'node:path';
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { RegisterPage } from '../pages/RegisterPage';

// Public demo/sandbox site; seeded accounts hold no real user data, so a
// fixed password in plaintext is fine here.
const SEEDED_USER_PASSWORD = 'Test1234!';

type MyWorkerFixtures = {
  workerStorageStatePath: string;
};

export const test = base.extend<{}, MyWorkerFixtures>({
  storageState: async ({ workerStorageStatePath }, use) => {
    await use(workerStorageStatePath);
  },
  workerStorageStatePath: [
    async ({ browser }, use, workerInfo) => {
      const fileName = resolve(
        workerInfo.project.outputDir,
        `.auth/${workerInfo.parallelIndex}.json`,
      );

      if (!existsSync(fileName)) {
        const page = await browser.newPage({
          baseURL: workerInfo.project.use.baseURL,
          storageState: undefined,
        });

        const username = `pw-worker-${workerInfo.parallelIndex}-${randomUUID().slice(0, 8)}`;
        const registerPage = new RegisterPage(page);
        await registerPage.goto();
        await registerPage.register(
          username,
          `${username}@example.com`,
          SEEDED_USER_PASSWORD,
        );
        await expect(registerPage.headerLoginLink).toBeHidden();

        mkdirSync(dirname(fileName), { recursive: true });
        await page.context().storageState({ path: fileName });
        await page.close();
      }

      await use(fileName);
    },
    { scope: 'worker' },
  ],
});

export { expect };
```

Notes:

- The username embeds `parallelIndex` (for readability in traces/network logs)
  plus a random suffix (so re-runs against a site that persists accounts
  forever never collide with a previous run's account).
- Only seed a new account when registration auto-logs the user in (verify
  this manually first). If it doesn't, log in with the same credentials right
  after registering.
- The account is cached for the worker's lifetime via `existsSync(fileName)`,
  same as the shared-login example — this is "one private account per worker",
  not "a new account for every single test".

## Example: page object fixtures

```typescript
// fixtures/index.ts
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoginPage } from '../pages/LoginPage';
import { DashboardPage } from '../pages/DashboardPage';

type MyFixtures = {
  loginPage: LoginPage;
  dashboardPage: DashboardPage;
};

export const test = base.extend<MyFixtures>({
  loginPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new LoginPage(page));
  },
  dashboardPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new DashboardPage(page));
  },
});

export { expect };
```

```typescript
// tests/login.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '../fixtures';

test('user can sign in', async ({ loginPage, dashboardPage }) => {
  await loginPage.goto();
  await loginPage.login(process.env.TEST_USER_EMAIL!, process.env.TEST_USER_PASSWORD!);
  await expect(dashboardPage.heading).toBeVisible();
});
```
